WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.729
In a local-first pattern, requests go to a local model unless capability, capacity, or availability requires an approved cloud alternative.

00:00:08.729 --> 00:00:12.180
This preserves local control for routine work.

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:16.038
The fallback must check data classification before forwarding.

00:00:16.038 --> 00:00:23.955
A sensitive request should fail safely or use a reduced local capability rather than cross a prohibited boundary.

00:00:23.955 --> 00:00:27.406
A cloud-first pattern can keep preprocessing local.

00:00:27.406 --> 00:00:35.729
Private documents may be searched, filtered, summarized, or redacted before an allowed subset is sent to a hosted model.

00:00:35.729 --> 00:00:44.865
Split retrieval and generation follows this idea: local systems retain source material and select passages, while another environment generates the answer.

00:00:44.865 --> 00:00:52.782
Local embeddings can prevent raw documents from leaving, but retrieved text and vector metadata still require policy review.

00:00:52.782 --> 00:00:59.481
Cloud GPUs can handle heavy batch jobs or temporary models while daily inference remains local.

00:00:59.481 --> 00:01:08.211
Edge inference places a model near sensors or users and sends only events, metrics, or approved results to centralized coordination.

00:01:08.211 --> 00:01:21.406
A private assistant may expose a local default model and allow users to choose an external model for approved tasks, making the boundary visible instead of hiding it in automatic routing.

00:01:21.406 --> 00:01:26.887
Each pattern needs explicit interfaces, identities, logs, retention, timeouts, and failure behavior.

00:01:26.887 --> 00:01:29.933
Minimize data movement and shared state.

00:01:29.933 --> 00:01:36.226
Begin with one justified split and test it end to end before adding routes.

00:01:36.226 --> 00:01:44.955
Patterns are not maturity levels; the simplest one that satisfies the workload is usually the easiest to secure and recover.
